WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:10.270 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in the morning of the 10th day of Rajab  eben Burak and his comrades returned to  the king of England while the Sultan  went off with his bodyguard and his  closest friends to the hill for the  butts on Scheffer Armour envoy's did not  cease to pass from one side to the other  in the hope of laying the foundation of  a firm peace these negotiations  continued to our men had procured the  money and the tale of the prisoners that  they were to deliver to the French at  the end of the first period in  accordance with the treaty the first  installment was to consist of the Holy  Cross 100,000 dinars and 1,600 prisoners  trustworthy men sent by the franks to  conduct the examination found it all  complete saving only the prisoners who  had been demanded by name all of whom  had not yet been gathered together and  thus the negotiations continued to drag  on till the end of the first term on  this day the 18th of Rajab the enemy  sent demanding what was due the Sultan  replied as follows choose one of two  things either send us back our comrades  and received the payment fixed for this  term in which case we will give hostages  to ensure the full execution of all that  is left or accept what we are going to  send you today and in your turn  give us hostages to keep until those of  our comrades whom you hold prisoners are  restored to this the end voice made  answer not so send us what is due for  this term and in return we will give our  solemn oath then your people shall be  restored you  [Music]  this proposition the Sultan rejected  knowing full well that if he were to  deliver the money the cross and the  prisoners while our men was still kept  captive by the Franks he would have no  security against treachery on the part  of the enemy and this would be a great  disaster to Islam then the King of  England seeing all the delays interposed  by the Sultan to the execution of the  treaty acted perfidiously as regards his  Muslim and prisoners on their yielding  the town he'd engaged to grant them life  adding that if the Sultan carried out  the bargain he would give them freedom  and suffer them to carry off their  children and wives if the Sultan did not  fulfil his engagements there were to be  made slaves now the King broke his  promises to them and made open display  of what he had till now kept hidden in  his heart by carrying out what he had  intended to do after he had received the  money and the Frank prisoners is thus  that people of his nation ultimately  admitted  [Music]  in the afternoon of Tuesday 27 Rajab at  4 o'clock he came out on horseback  towards the Frankish army knights  footmen turned and advanced to the pits  at the foot  to which place he had already planted  his tents the Franks on reaching the  middle of the plain that stretches  between this hill and that of Kasaan  close to which place the Sultan's  advanced guard had drawn back ordered  all the Muslim and prisoners whose  martyrdom God had decreed for this day  to be brought before him they numbered  more than 3000 and were all bound with  ropes the franks then flung themselves  upon them all at once and massacred them  with sword and lance in cold blood  our advanced guard had already told the  Sultan of the enemy's movements and he  scented some reinforcements but only  after the massacre the muzzle man's  seeing what was being done to the  prisoners rushed against the Franks and  in the combat which lasted till  nightfall several was slain and wounded  on each side on the morrow morning how  people gathered at the spot and found  the muzzle man stretched out upon the  ground as martyrs for the faith they  even recognized some of the dead and the  sight was a great affliction to them the  enemy had only spared the prisoners of  note and such as were strong enough to  work the motives of this Massacre are  differently told according to some the  captives were slain by way of reprisal  for the death of those Christians whom  the muzzle Mane's had slain others again  say that the King of England thought it  unwise to leave so many in the town  after his departure God alone knows what  the real reason was  [Music]  you

